// A contract member is BODILESS, so it cannot be `unsafe` — and a contract could not usefully specify
// one anyway. A member is a CONDUIT: both ends are already closed by its own types. An implementation
// whose signature names `UnsafePtr` must be an `unsafe fn`, and a caller cannot invoke one without
// holding an `UnsafePtr`. That is exactly what keeps `A: Allocator` a perfectly safe BOUND while
// `allocate`/`deallocate` stay uninvocable outside an `unsafe fn`.
type contract RawSink for value {
    unsafe fn void put(UnsafePtr<int32> p);
}

fn int32 main() { return 0; }
